# libcss: :not() takes a selector list (patch 328)
# Generated 2026-10-29
# Base: seleron/vitabrowser (patch stack through 327)
#
# libcss's :not() accepted one simple selector (CSS 3). A compound
# (:not(.a.b)), a list (:not(.a, .b)) or a complex selector (:not(.a .b))
# invalidated the rule. WordPress's navigation sheet resets the closed
# menu container with
# `.wp-block-navigation__responsive-container:not(.is-menu-open.is-menu-open){background-color:inherit!important}`.
# That rule was dropped, so the global header's hover colour painted a
# dark box over the whole wordpress.org navigation bar.
#
#  - parse/language.c parsePseudo(): parseNotSimple() probes for the old
#    one-simple-selector form (it allocates nothing, so abandoning the
#    probe is free), and that form keeps its existing detail. Anything
#    else parses with parseSelectorArgList() into a SELLIST detail, as
#    :is() does since patch 91, with negate set. match_detail() already
#    inverts any negated detail, so matching needs no change. Specificity
#    is the most specific argument's (Selectors 4), as for :is().
#  - parseSelectorArgList() takes a forgiving flag. :is() and :where()
#    stay forgiving. :not() is not, as in Chromium: one invalid
#    alternative (a pseudo-element, anything this libcss lacks)
#    invalidates the rule. An alternative using a pseudo-class this
#    libcss parses but never matches (:has(), :host, :host-context(),
#    also inside a nested :is()) is rejected too. Negated, it would match
#    every element (fixes360b rejects them in the simple form for the
#    same reason).
#
# Gate: CSS-not-list (NOTLIST328, 10 legs: compound, list, complex,
# type+class, the WordPress !important case against a more specific
# rule, :not(:is()), id specificity from a list argument, a
# pseudo-element invalidating the rule, :not(:not())). Without this patch
# it reads 1/10. Only the invalid-rule leg passes, because the old parser
# dropped that rule too.
